  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Four different bells toll every $1\\frac{1}{2}$ hours, $1\\frac{1}{3}$ hours, $1\\frac{1}{4}$ hours, and $1\\frac{1}{5}$ hours. All four bells toll at midday. At what time of day do they next toll simultaneously?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Midnight, two and a half days later.\n\nThe LCM of the four intervals is $60$ hours.",
      "worked": "Express the intervals in minutes:\n$$\\begin{align*}1\\frac12\\times60&=90\\\\1\\frac13\\times60&=80\\\\1\\frac14\\times60&=75\\\\1\\frac15\\times60&=72.\\end{align*}$$\nTheir prime factorisations are $90=2\\times3^2\\times5$, $80=2^4\\times5$, $75=3\\times5^2$ and $72=2^3\\times3^2$. Take the greatest power of each prime:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{LCM}(90,80,75,72)&=2^4\\times3^2\\times5^2\\\\\\operatorname{LCM}(90,80,75,72)&=3600\\text{ minutes}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $3600\\div60=60$ hours, the bells next toll together $2$ days and $12$ hours after midday: midnight, two and a half days later.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 96
  },
